{"kind":"report","text":"C1 sequence ruling: the slot comes first and holds the deadline; no multi-item opening\n\nThis replaces step 2 of 78100b55, which assumed that one act opens two items. It does not. Section 7 already names the right order: \"the owner plans a month, a post per slot with a channel, a date and a brief.\" Make the slot an item, open it before the post, and put the deadline on it.\n\n1. `schedule` (grant publish.plan) opens a slot item with its channel and date. Its effects set `warnAt` from the date minus 172,800 seconds with the new time form, and set `dueAt` to the date. The slot starts in waiting.\n2. `plan` opens the post. It names its slot by a field of type item, `also: { slot: { item: \"slot\", by: \"slot\" } }`, and stores it in a fixed reference slot. One guard requires the slot to be waiting or warned. Another, a `none` range, refuses a second post in the same slot. The post reads its date from its slot. It keeps no copy.\n3. Two timed rules apply to the slot: waiting to warned at `warnAt`, and waiting or warned to overdue at `dueAt`. Each notifies the owner.\n4. `approve` reaches the slot through the post's reference, `via: { slot: \"slot\", of: \"on\" }`, and moves it to cleared. The admitted result clause moves it to done. Overdue still fires for a cleared slot whose post is not admitted by the date.\n\nAtomicity needs no setup state. An interrupted sequence leaves a slot with no post. That is a true state that the owner should see, and the rule warns about it two days before the date, as it should. It never leaves a post without a deadline, because `plan` needs its slot.\n\nBounds: at most 24 live slots, and still at most 24 posts.\n\nIf a result clause cannot change an item reached through the act's reference, stop and say so. That is the one place this sequence might need more.\n\nPut the shared time-form commit first, as ruled. Then reorder the calendar commits onto it."}